# Packages strapd installs from the AUR, kept apart from strapd-base.packages
# because they are built on the target rather than pulled from a repo.
#
# Only the hardware-conditional ones are left here. Everything else strapd needs
# from the AUR is vendored: its PKGBUILD lives in arch/vendor/ and is built by
# `strapd vendor build`, which install.sh runs. See vendor/manifest.tsv for that
# set and vendor/README.md for why.
#
# What is listed below is installed by install/hardware/*, only on a machine
# that matches, through strapd-pkg-add -> strapd-pkg-aur-add. It is not vendored
# because none of it is wanted on a machine without the hardware, and a DKMS
# tree or an NVIDIA driver is a large thing to carry in the repo for the small
# number of machines that need it.
#
# These do need yay, which is vendored, so it exists by the time any of this
# runs. On a machine where it does not, strapd-pkg-aur-add says so and the
# hardware pass reports that one quirk rather than dying.

# --- ASUS ROG laptops ---
asusctl

# --- NVIDIA cards too old for the open kernel modules (no GSP) ---
nvidia-580xx-dkms
nvidia-580xx-utils
lib32-nvidia-580xx-utils

# --- Apple MacBooks with an SPI keyboard ---
macbook12-spi-driver-dkms

# --- TUXEDO / Clevo / Slimbook keyboard backlight ---
tuxedo-drivers-nocompatcheck-dkms

# --- Motorcomm YT6801 ethernet ---
yt6801-dkms

# --- Framework 16 QMK HID ---
qmk-hid

# --- Not currently installable ---
# yaru-icon-theme was here, and install/config/theme-system.sh and
# install/user/first-run/gnome-theme.sh both still expect /usr/share/icons/Yaru.
# Its AUR git repo is empty as of the last vendor refresh (the package page
# resolves, the repo has no refs), so there is nothing to install or to vendor.
# The icon lookups fall back rather than fail, so this is a cosmetic gap, but it
# is a gap: yaru-icon-theme-git is empty too, and no other package provides it.
